['```cpp
#include <list>
#include <algorithm>

void insertAfter(std::forward_list<std::string>& events, const std::string& key, const std::string& value) {
    auto it = events.begin();
    auto prev_it = it;

    while (it != events.end() && *it != key) {
        prev_it = it++;
    }

    if (it == events.end()) {
        events.push_back(value);
    } else {
        auto next_it = std::next(it);
        events.insert(it, value);
        prev_it = next_it; // Move the previous iterator to the newly inserted node
    }
}
```

This function modifies the given forward list in place by inserting the second argument after the first occurrence of the first argument. If the first argument is not found, it will be appended to the end of the list. Keep in mind that this function does not handle any error conditions or performance optimizations for your distributed system as specified in the task description. For those requirements, you may need to use more sophisticated data structures and design patterns such as threading, caching, and distributed systems libraries.']

