[' I will provide you a base structure for your asynchronous C# function using `Task` and `SemaphoreSlim` for concurrency and throttling, `HttpClient` for HTTP requests, and a simple retry mechanism with exponential backoff. Note that this is a simplified version; you'll need to adjust it according to your specific requirements and the CMS API details.

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Concurrent;
using System.Net.Http;
using System.Threading;
using System.Threading.Tasks;
using Microsoft.Extensions.Logging;

public class HttpRequestManager
{
    private readonly SemaphoreSlim _semaphore;
    private readonly HttpClient _httpClient;
    private readonly ILogger<HttpRequestManager> _logger;
    private readonly int _maxConcurrentRequests;
    private readonly TimeSpan _backoffDelay;

    public HttpRequestManager(ILogger<HttpRequestManager> logger, HttpClient httpClient, int maxConcurrentRequests = 10000, TimeSpan backoffDelay = TimeSpan.FromSeconds(2))
    {
        _logger = logger;
        _httpClient = httpClient;
        _maxConcurrentRequests = maxConcurrentRequests;
        _backoffDelay = backoffDelay;
        _semaphore = new SemaphoreSlim(_maxConcurrentRequests);
    }

    public async Task SendRequestAsync(string requestId, string url, HttpContent content, CancellationToken cancellationToken)
    {
        await _semaphore.WaitAsync(cancellationToken);
        try
        {
            using var httpResponse = await _httpClient.PostAsync(url, content, cancellationToken);
            if (!httpResponse.IsSuccessStatusCode)
            {
                RetryRequest(requestId, url, content, _backoffDelay, cancellationToken);
            }
        }
        catch (Exception ex)
        {
            LogError(requestId, url, ex);
        }
        finally
        {
            _semaphore.Release();
        }
    }

    private void RetryRequest(string requestId, string url, HttpContent content, TimeSpan delay, CancellationToken cancellationToken)
    {
        if (cancellationToken.IsCancellationRequested) return;

        var backoffDelay = delay * (uint)Math.Pow(2, (double)Math.Min(3, Math.Max(0, _retryAttempts + 1)));
        _logger.LogInformation("Retrying request {RequestId} after {BackoffDelay} due to transient error.", requestId, backoffDelay);

        Task.Delay(backoffDelay).ContinueWith(_ => SendRequestAsync(requestId, url, content, cancellationToken), TaskContinuationOptions.NotOnCanceled | TaskContinuationOptions.ExecuteSynchronously);
    }

    private void LogError(string requestId, string url, Exception ex)
    {
        _logger.LogError(ex, "Failed to process request {RequestId} at {Url}", requestId, url);
    }
}
```

In this example, the `HttpRequestManager` class takes an `ILogger`, `HttpClient`, max concurrent requests, and backoff delay as constructor parameters. The `SendRequestAsync` function handles a single HTTP POST request while ensuring concurrency using the SemaphoreSlim class. The retry mechanism is based on a simple exponential backoff strategy with a circuit breaker pattern.

You may also consider implementing additional features like request prioritization, backpressure mechanisms, and real-time metric monitoring to handle spikes in traffic efficiently. Additionally, remember to test your solution thoroughly using unit tests and integration tests to ensure reliability under various scenarios.']

